div.div:
ŏlīvētum , i, n. id.,
I.a place planted with olive-trees, an olive-grove (class.): “quo pluris sint nostra oliveta,” Cic. Rep. 3, 9, 16 : “vineta, segetes, oliveta,” id. N. D. 3, 36, 86 ;
Varr. R. R. 1, 22, 3; Col. 3, 11, 3; 5, 9, 1 sq.: veteris proverbii meminisse convenit, eum qui aret olivetum, rogare fructum; qui stercoret, exorare; “qui caedat, cogere,” id. 5, 9, 15 : “facere,” to plant, Calp. Fragm. 6, 16 : “vineta et oliveta,” Vulg. Deut. 6, 11 .—Hence, Mons Oliveti, the mountain of Olivet, or of the olive-grove, east of Jerusalem (= mons Olivarum, v. oliva), Vulg. Matt. 21, 1; 26, 30; id. Act. 1, 12.
